# Supabase Conventions

## Client Setup
- Use the `@supabase/ssr` package for Next.js App Router (not `@supabase/supabase-js` directly on the server)
- Create two clients:
  - `src/lib/supabase/client.ts` — browser client (for client components)
  - `src/lib/supabase/server.ts` — server client using cookies (for server components and API routes)

```ts
// src/lib/supabase/server.ts
import { createServerClient } from "@supabase/ssr"
import { cookies } from "next/headers"

export function createClient() {
  const cookieStore = cookies()
  return createServerClient(
    process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL!,
    process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY!,
    { cookies: { getAll: () => cookieStore.getAll(), setAll: (c) => c.forEach(({ name, value, options }) => cookieStore.set(name, value, options)) } }
  )
}
```

## Environment Variables
Required in `.env.local`:
```
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=   # server-only, never expose to client
```

## Authentication
- Auth is handled by Supabase Auth (email/password for members)
- Session is managed via cookies (SSR-compatible)
- Always check auth in Server Components / middleware — never trust client-side only
- Roles stored in `profiles` table: `role: "member" | "admin"`

```ts
// Protect a server component
const supabase = createClient()
const { data: { user } } = await supabase.auth.getUser()
if (!user) redirect("/login")
```

## Database Conventions
- Table names: `snake_case`, plural (e.g. `events`, `donations`, `profiles`)
- Always use Row Level Security (RLS) — never disable it
- Members can only read/write their own rows unless `role = 'admin'`
- Use Supabase-generated TypeScript types from `supabase gen types typescript`; store in `src/types/supabase.ts`

## Planned Tables

| Table | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `profiles` | Extended user info (name, role, membership status) |
| `events` | Synagogue events and Jewish holidays |
| `donations` | Member donation records |
| `study_materials` | Torah study content and parasha resources |

## Query Patterns
- Prefer server-side queries in Server Components over client-side fetching
- Always handle Supabase errors explicitly — never ignore `error` from queries

```ts
// ✅ GOOD
const { data, error } = await supabase.from("events").select("*")
if (error) {
  console.error("[events] query failed:", error.message)
  return null
}
```
